From: mscharged-port
Subject: Report QR2's public IP as the Wii's decimal natively
Upstream-base: 9046e67aa2077a7eb4ed6e28c9df3c1d3b368f84
Classification: native byte-order compatibility

QR2 learns its public address from the master server as hex, keeps it in
network order through htonl, and reports it back in each heartbeat as the
decimal of that u32. On the Wii htonl changes nothing, so the decimal is
the address's big-endian value, which the server compares with the
address it sees. Natively htonl swaps the bytes and the heartbeat reported
another number: the server logged "Public IP mismatch" and dropped the
heartbeat.

Natively the heartbeat reports ntohl(publicip), the Wii's number; the
stored address keeps network order for its other users.

diff --git a/src/RVL_SDK/gamespy/qr2/qr2.c b/src/RVL_SDK/gamespy/qr2/qr2.c
index bec1464..33eb2dd 100644
--- a/src/RVL_SDK/gamespy/qr2/qr2.c
+++ b/src/RVL_SDK/gamespy/qr2/qr2.c
@@ -1503,7 +1503,13 @@ static void send_heartbeat(qr2_t qrec, int statechanged) {
   qr2_buffer_addA(&buf, qrec->gamename);
   if (qrec->pa_callback) {
     qr2_buffer_addA(&buf, "publicip");
+#if defined(MSCHARGED_NATIVE)
+    // publicip is in network order. The server checks the decimal of its
+    // big-endian value, which a native load gives only after ntohl.
+    qr2_buffer_add_int(&buf, (int)ntohl(qrec->publicip));
+#else
     qr2_buffer_add_int(&buf, (int)qrec->publicip);
+#endif
     qr2_buffer_addA(&buf, "publicport");
     qr2_buffer_add_int(&buf, qrec->publicport);
   }
